      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14155852/assets/13822734ec2aa7810758b2aa3fd6ddd9972fdeb37d5aa2afaa796419f7327976.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find ${f}^{′}(1.5).$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute ${f}^{′}(1.5),$ we need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at $x=1.5.$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ on $[0,2].$ Notice that $x=1.5$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14155852/assets/2ddcaccc6d4b626001ea8179faabc9b298956ad5d7cce2461e6e8c3a4fb7754f.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in (0,2)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{0.5-2}{2-0}=\\frac{-1.5}{2}=-0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(1.5)=-0.75.$",
